 ],
 "literal": "Listen! May you live, friend! Today he / if he does not come, it is good indeed! On the slope / where small millet has ripened, in the wide, dark field, / because of those who reap at night, the small Toṇṭakam drum / even at midnight will sound, / as the watchmen of the night do not sleep.",
 "translation": "Listen, my friend, may you live long! If he does not come tonight, it is for the best. In the wide, dark field on the mountain slope where the small millet has ripened, the small Toṇṭakam drum will keep sounding even at midnight, because of those who reap the harvest at night, keeping the watchmen wide awake.",
 "modern": "தோழியே, கேள்! நீ வாழ்க! இன்று அவர் வராமல் இருந்தால் மிகவும் நல்லது. மலைச்சாரலில் சிறிய தினை விளைந்த அகன்ற கரிய புனத்தில், இரவில் தினை அறுப்பவர்கள் இருப்பதனால், நள்ளிரவிலும் தொண்டகச் சிறுபறை ஒலித்துக் கொண்டே இருக்கும்; இதனால் இரவுக்காவலர்கள் தூங்காமல் விழித்திருப்பார்கள்.",
 "explanation": "The friend is speaking to the heroine, warning her that the hero should not visit for a secret night tryst tonight. She explains that the small millet in the wide mountain fields has ripened, prompting people to harvest it even during the night. Because of these night-reapers, the watchmen are beating their Toṇṭakam drums and staying alert and sleepless throughout the midnight hours. This makes a secret meeting highly dangerous. By highlighting the sleepless watchmen, the friend subtly urges the hero to stop the secret visits and seek a formal, public marriage.",
